# Chennai District DMK Conference
Tamil title: சென்னை மாவட்ட தி.மு.க. மாநாடு

This address was delivered by Arignar Anna at the Chennai District DMK Conference held in Napier Park on 11 and 12 July 1953.

Beloved Chairman, comrades and mothers,

I have been invited to speak as this conference, conducted pleasantly for two days, approaches its conclusion. Until now, my dear comrades, who possess the ability to offer analytical counsel about the welfare of the country while showing accord with the world, have poured out angry feeling today instead of analytical speeches. The reason is that Rajagopalachari imprisoned comrade Kannabiran, who was to welcome all of us gathered to exchange views at this conference, comrade Aasaithambi, who came to participate, and sixty-five other comrades.

Suppose a man's wife is suffering from an incurable illness. He runs to a doctor for help and pleads, “It is urgent, sir! Do not neglect her!” If the doctor replies, “Wait a little; I am only now putting on my tie,” the petitioner may, overcome by anger, even strike the doctor. We have heard of such events in the country.

Likewise, the arrested comrades performed the ordinary task of explaining the danger facing education—or, in good Tamil, went to submit a proper representation. The Chief Minister subjected them to repression. However celebrated a person may be for serenity, and however great a “saint” he may be at controlling his mind, he certainly cannot tolerate this authoritarian conduct. The war drums sounded by our friends Natarajan, Sampath and Nedunchezhiyan are its echo.

We had calmly explained the method of struggle. If today we have been driven to the level of “turmoil,” it is because ten days ago we gathered and demonstrated to Acharya the opposition of everyone, yet to this day he continues to treat us with contempt.

After we announced a picket before Rajaji's house, only today has Periyar passed a resolution that the Dravidar Kazhagam too will picket before the Legislature on the fourteenth. The difference between us and them is that they can do only one thing at a time, while we can demonstrate a two-front struggle at the same moment. I can devise certain campaign plans even while sitting in a cinema.

Periyar's son Sampath stands before the Chief Minister's house, while Periyar's party struggles before the Legislature. Before the father can act, the son blocks Acharya at his house—perhaps because he thinks that while we are here, why should father be troubled?

At the founding of the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam, I said that although we had separated—or been driven away—the two organisations were a double-barrelled gun. One shot before Rajagopalachari's house, another before the Legislature. Will the elderly leader come to terms before those shots are fired?

I rejoice inwardly at the battle hymns sung today by the younger brothers at this political conference. I possess unshakeable confidence that they will certainly succeed in the righteous struggle. This is not divination. I have lived closely with their intelligence, enthusiasm and unwavering ideal; therefore, I have no doubt that the moral struggle will yield victory. But I am certain it will be severe and prolonged. I fear whether the enthusiasm of the beginning can be sustained until victory.

Initial enthusiasm resembles the water of a sudden river flood: it surges and roams, then diminishes. I do not desire such floodwater enthusiasm. I desire a long, steady reservoir—enthusiasm that never dries up. I ask that the rousing spirit gained today continue for a long time without losing strength or changing direction.

There are many kinds of snakes. Some move in curves; some rise and spread their hoods. Acharya resembles a green snake, hiding among green leaves and striking from concealment. He will introduce an unjust scheme and simultaneously flash a smile. Wherever he goes, he cheerfully says, “Periyar is my friend,” while covertly attacking the programme Periyar advances.

Machiavelli of Italy said, “If you wish to rule a country dictatorially and accomplish whatever you desire, you must be both a lion and a little fox.” Acharya is such a man. That is why I feared at the outset that the struggle would be prolonged.

Likewise, the struggle certainly will not cease until Dalmiapuram becomes Kallakudi. Without allowing the courage gained today to weaken, we shall conduct it without violence.

I appointed comrade Karunanidhi leader of the Dalmiapuram struggle because I heard many mock him: “Comrade Karunanidhi is an artist; he can display his skill only in stories and brandish only a cardboard sword in an imaginary picture.” Before becoming an artist, Karunanidhi was an actor; before becoming an actor, he was a student of the ideal. He not only worked in all three fields but suffered for them.

A play written and performed by Karunanidhi was staged in Pondicherry. During ensuing disorder, he was beaten and thrown into a Pondicherry street. I appointed him leader of the Dalmiapuram struggle so the country would know my confidence that his commitment was not confined to story and imagination: once he takes up a duty and places his foot forward, he will not withdraw it.

At this very moment, the executive committee for that struggle is meeting in Tiruchirappalli. If Karunanidhi is arrested, the next commander, comrade A. P. Chitrarasu, is there. Here, if comrade Sampath is arrested, the struggle will continue under comrade N. V. Natarajan. Two struggles, and this conference between them: it is entirely fitting to say that it sings a battle hymn.

I observed that the anger and agitation among the speakers and among you became somewhat greater when they condemned Acharya's pernicious education scheme than when other matters emerged. There is no need for rage. We are not hasty people, and we have spoken of our strength.

We descend from the Three Crowned Kings; no one denies it. Our ancestors bore sword and spear upon the battlefield; literary evidence exists. We are warriors and people of discernment, the lineage of a tiger that never shows its back. History gives this testimony.

But what kind of opponent do we face, and what is the strength of his forces? I do not mean the Chief Minister's prisons, guns, lathis and prohibitory orders. I am confident that our comrades will give anything to make the ideal victorious. But Acharya possesses great newspapers that impose a blackout, and his followers have become worthy gentlemen who distort our struggle.

Mothers bless us as we go to the field—forgive me, they send us on our way; no, they do not merely send us—forgive me, they have set out declaring, “We too shall certainly come.”

I have learned that Acharya and his associates intend to say: “Whether men or women come, I shall not regard them. If they pull the handle, I shall remove on 15 July the apparatus fitted to stop the train when it is pulled. Will the train not run?” If he adopts that method and disregards our righteous struggle, I now designate the twentieth as another day to stop the trains. Will he remove the apparatus again? A month has thirty days. If he uses the same method on all thirty, the railway system may change, but our method of struggle certainly will not. What greater humiliation could a Government require?

Sir, we do not seek to destroy you, overturn a train or tear up the rails. We seek to stop a train—not at night, but in daylight—to explain to the public the danger of Acharya's education scheme. We seek to ask whether it is wise for an uncrowned king, forgetting his responsibility, qualifications and position, to call the great agitators of an entire subcontinent fools and childish. That is the purpose of the moral struggle.

If he declares that he will run the train after removing the power of the chain linking engine and carriages to stop it when pulled, what name should be given to such political cowardice?

Imagine a thief writes to say, “I shall rob your house tonight.” If you immediately vacate the house, what does that mean? What should you be called? What greater distinction could a party possess?

We do not come to steal. DMK volunteers will lie upon the railway track in groups of ten to recover lost honour. Will Rajagopalachari himself come to drive the train over them?

There is one field at Kallakudi and another struggle at Mambalam. Although they are separated by a great distance, they resemble the strings of a veena: the strings stand apart, yet when a finger plucks them they join in producing sweet music. Likewise, the victory of these two struggles will further strengthen our fundamental question.

We have not entered two fields simultaneously merely to win cries of “Bravo,” in expectation that someone on the shore will shake our hands, or in the belief that repression will be mild.

[A short line in the source is illegible in OCR.]

We gave them Thalamuthu and Natarajan; they returned them as corpses. Can this be called an ordinary struggle? I saw with my own eyes the time in Kumbakonam when I was needed as dictator of the campaign to defy a Section 144 prohibitory order.

Comrades would carry a flag and cry, “Down with the prohibitory order.” The police vans would arrive. The police would say they must not proceed along the street, but our friends continued. The lathis immediately spoke. Comrades fell, but they did not turn and flee. They swam in a river of blood. In the end, the police tired from striking; the campaigners did not run.

One day my friend Police Inspector Adithan came to me. With tears waiting to fall from his eyes, he asked, “Annadurai, if we are to strike your volunteers, must we not close our eyes to do it? Even when their skulls split and blood flows, they fall crying only ‘Thalamuthu! Natarajan!’ Why did you organise this struggle in the town where I serve?”

I told him: “You strike because it is your duty. When the red water flowing from our volunteer's skull joins the tears welling in the eyes of you, the guardian constrained by law, only then will a new society be born.”

In the same manner, Rajagopalachari may unleash more police and the blows may fall harder. We must endure; we are accustomed to endurance. When struck, there must be no cowardly cry of “Father! Mother! Alas!” Only the names of those great warriors, “Thalamuthu! Natarajan!” must be uttered.

As blow follows blow, the cry must change into: “Down with the varnashrama education scheme! Let knowledge grow!” We need a mind unshaken by prison or torture. A struggle is not like going to pluck flowers; it is like walking barefoot through a harsh forest of cactus thorns.

This struggle is not flower-picking—hold that in your minds. If you have resolved to enter the field, it is truly as though you offer yourselves without considering the sweetness of life.

At first he will be arrogant. During the anti-Hindi agitation, this same Acharya used a degrading method to imprison mothers. Our leader Panneerselvam, later swallowed by the Arabian Sea, asked him in the Legislature, “Why, sir, should mothers be thrown into prison?” Acharya mocked them in reply: “They cannot afford milk for their children, so they have come into prison.”

Our Selvam asked, “Was it just to throw Natarajan into prison?”

“He was uneducated,” Acharya replied.

Panneerselvam asked, “Was it right to send Periyar Ramasamy to Bellary Prison?”

“The Bellary climate suits Periyar's body,” Acharya answered, brimming with humour.

It is a Chief Minister possessing such arrogance whom we oppose.

Remember: we oppose a “Machiavelli.” Keep it in your minds. Think how we once arose like winged termites.

With confidence in our hearts, firm commitment to honesty and dignity as our armour, let us set out toward the field. This insubstantial education scheme must either be abolished or withdrawn tomorrow itself.

MLAs will oppose the new education scheme in the Legislature, while the public hurry to take their places in the ranks. Acharya is about to encounter the first storm of his life. He arrogantly imagines he has climbed to a great summit. Poor man, he has forgotten that he stands upon a mound of sand. He must now slide down and fall.

When the DMK organises you, it means the motherland calls you. It means the country for which it labours calls its owners. If we cannot wipe away Dalmia's name with sweat, we stand ready to wash it away with blood.

The battle hymn has been sung to the world, the war drum sounded to the town and the warning sent to the rulers.

Nehru spoke contemptuously of Tamils. Treating Tamil struggles as trivial, he called them foolish according to his own whim.

Nehru abused us. Before we could oppose that unbearable news, Syngman Rhee, the South Korean leader, abused Nehru: “You are a coward, with a mind as cowardly as a little chick's and the intelligence of a monkey.”

I do not praise Rhee for having sung a hymn on our behalf. We grieve that a good man should receive such an invocation from the South Korean leader. The man who sowed the deed has been made to reap it. It is not that we cannot scatter those evil words back at him; our anguish is simply that a great leader should be addressed in this manner.

Therefore, a Tamil will not answer abuse with abuse and slander with slander in speech. Let this conference teach Pandit Nehru a lesson: “Behold our method in action; behold our ranks.” That is my request.

---NOTES---
The OCR prints the conference dates as “11, 72-7-1953”; the two-day context and heading support 11 and 12 July 1953. The source is retained verbatim.

The speech concerns the 1953 campaigns against C. Rajagopalachari's education scheme and against the replacement of the name Kallakudi by Dalmiapuram. Its repeated railway references distinguish a public nonviolent train-stopping action from derailment or clandestine sabotage.

The source's “காஸ்கள்” in the train-chain passage is uncertain, possibly referring to a gas, valve or other brake apparatus. The translation uses the functional description supplied by the surrounding text rather than naming an unverified component.

The footnote identifies Sir A. T. Panneerselvam and says the fate of the aircraft carrying him to take office in London remains unknown. Historical names and rhetoric are translated in source context without silent revision.
